In France, the voting process has traditionally been relatively low-tech: voters individually separate themselves in voting booths, mark their preferred candidate on a pre-printed paper sheet, and then place it into an envelope.
After the official verifies the voter's identity, the voter places the ballot paper in the ballot box and signs the voting register.
French electoral law strictly regulates these procedures.
Ballot boxes must be transparent after 1988, so that voters and observers can see that no ballot was present at the beginning of voting and no additional ballot was added except for those of authorized voters, which have been properly counted.
Candidates have the right to send their representatives to oversee every aspect of the process. In the evening, the poll workers conduct the vote counting under strict supervision and specific regulations.
