They can see well and move quietly forward in the dark through night vision. The savanna cheetah blends in with its surroundings and launches attacks on its prey.
When a small group of organisms (a small population) becomes separated from a larger population (for example, if they move across a mountain range or a river, or if they move to a new island), and they can no longer return easily, they will often find themselves in a different environment than the one they previously inhabited.
In a new environment, there are diverse resources and different competitors. Therefore, populations will require different features or adaptations for vigorous competition, as compared to what they previously needed.
There has been no change at all in the original population; they still require the same level of adaptation as before.
As time passes, as the new settlement adapts to its new environment, it gradually begins to resemble other settlements.
Eventually, thousands or perhaps millions of years later, these two populations will appear so different that they will no longer be considered part of the same species.
We call this process speciation, which means the formation of only new species. Speciation is an inevitable consequence and a very important part of evolution.
