The Great Pyramid of Giza is the only one of the Seven Wonders that still stands in its original location today.
The Great Pyramid, built by the Egyptians in the third century BCE, is one of several large pyramidal structures constructed in honor of deceased pharaohs.
The Giza Plateau, also known as "Giza Necropolis," located in the Valley of the Dead in Egypt, includes numerous pyramids (the Great Pyramid being the largest), several smaller tombs, various temples, and the Great Sphinx.
The Great Pyramid was built in honor of the Pharaoh Khufu, and many smaller pyramids, tombs, and temples were constructed in honor of Khufu's wives and members of his family.
