The theory of technological determinism is a term that practically encompasses a wide range of views, from those that suggest technological advancement or the inevitability of modern technology, to the strong sense that human destiny is governed by an underlying logic embodied in scientific laws and technology.
Most explanations of technological determinism share two general notions: that technological development follows a course that is independent of cultural or political influences, and conversely, that technology exerts effects on societies that are inherited rather than socially conditioned.
For example, one could say that a motorcar necessarily smoothens the way for road development.
However, it is not economically viable to lay a network of roads across the country just for a few vehicles; therefore, new production methods have been introduced to reduce the cost of vehicle ownership.
Large-scale car ownership also leads to a high rate of accidents on roads, prompting the development of new techniques in the health sector to repair damaged bodies.
A major element in Romanticism is cultural determination, derived from writers such as Goethe, Gottlieb Fichte, and Schiller.
In the context of romanticism, geography shaped individuals, and over time, traditions and cultures associated with that geography, which were in harmony with the society's setting, proved to be more suitable than rigid legal codes.
Just as Paris is known today as a center of fashion, Constantinople was regarded as the center of fashion in feudal Europe.
It is known to have begun around 400 CE as a center of pleasure and luxury, and it remained in existence until the 1100s CE.
The main reason for its decline in the twelfth century was that the Crusaders returned home carrying silks and spices as gifts, which were worth more than the prices offered in Byzantine markets.
This was the time when the title of fashion capital shifted from Constantinople to Paris.
The Gothic style flourished between the 10th and 11th centuries and the 14th century.
Clothing was initially greatly influenced by Byzantine culture in the East.
However, due to the slow communication system, the West could lag behind by 25 to 30 years.
In the late Middle Ages, Western Europe began to develop its own style. One of the most significant developments at the time was the popularization of the practice of using buttons to fasten the garments of the Crusaders.
Subsistence agriculture is farming carried out to produce food only up to the extent required to meet the needs of the farmer and his family.
Sustainable agriculture is simple, often organic, and achieves maximum production by using locally adapted, environmentally safe seeds in combination with crop rotation or other relatively simple practices.
Historically, most farmers have been engaged in agriculture for subsistence, and this remains the case in many developing countries even today.
Subcultures bring together like-minded individuals who feel alienated through societal standards and provide them with a legitimate means to develop a sense of identity.
Subcultures may be specific to certain age, race, class, location, and/or gender groups.
The characteristics that distinguish subcultures may include a combination of linguistic, aesthetic, religious, political, sexual, geographical, or other factors.
Members of subcultures often indicate their membership through distinctive and symbolic styles, such as body fashion, specific dress styles, and unique speech patterns.
One of the most common ways used to highlight social significance is to identify, during their upbringing, a few unpleasant incidents in children's lives in which they were ignored by adults, deliberately or accidentally subjected to mistreatment, and prevented from participating in social activities.
Such children are called "feral" or wild. Some feral children are confined by people (usually their parents); in some cases, the reason for the child being abandoned is that the parents reject the child's severe mental or physical disability.
The wild children must have endured severe abuse or trauma before being left behind or running away.
Other individuals are reportedly raised by animals; some are said to have been living in the jungle in their own dens.
When reared completely by non-human animals, wild children (within psychological limits) exhibit behavior similar to that of animals raised completely, showing fear of humans or indifference toward them.
