It is difficult for an ape-like digestive system in the open field to meet its amino acid requirements from available plant resources.
Moreover, failure to do so may have dangerous consequences: reduced growth, malnutrition, and ultimately death.
The leaves and seeds of plants, which are the most easily available resources, contain protein, but these are difficult to digest for creatures like us until they are cooked.
In contrast, animal food (ants, termites, eggs) is not only easier to digest, but also contains high levels of protein and all essential amino acids.
We should not be surprised if our own ancestors solved their "protein problem" in much the same way that langurs do today, across vast areas.
