After being adopted by the Congress on July 4 through a handwritten draft signed by Congress President John Hancock and Secretary Charles Thomson, it was then sent a few blocks away to John Dunlap's printing shop.
Throughout the night, copies were made between 150 and 200, which is now known as "Dunlap Broadside."
The first reading of the document took place on July 8 in the courtyard of Independence Hall, conducted by John Nicolson.
A letter was sent to George Washington on 6 July, which he had read aloud to his troops in New York on 9 July. A copy of it reached London on 10 August.
The 25 D'Nelly Broadside versions whose existence is still known are the oldest surviving copies of this document. The original manuscript has been lost.
