The Hershey-Chase experiment was the most important in identifying DNA as the genetic material.
Hershey and Chase used bacteria, or viruses, to carry out the insertion of their DNA into a bacterium.
They conducted two experiments: one to produce DNA from phage using radioactive phosphorus, and the other to produce protein from phage using radioactive sulfur.
Depending on its type, the significance of the affected genetic material, and whether the affected cells are malignant, the alteration can produce various effects.
Only alterations occurring in germ line cells can be passed on to children, whereas alterations in any other location may cause cell death or cancer.
