Virtual teams adhere to the same standards of quality as their well-known counterparts, but there are also subtle differences between the two.
Virtual team members often perform the role of contacting their immediate physical group.
They have greater autonomy than traditional teams because their teams can meet across different time zones, which their local management cannot understand.
The presence of a genuine "invisible team" (Larsen and Lafasto, 1989, p. 109) is also a distinctive feature of a virtual team.
The invisible team is the team to which every member is required to report. The invisible team sets the standard for every member.
