The foundation of the modern city of Casablanca was laid by the Berber Phoenicians in the 10th century BCE, and it was subsequently used as a strategic port by the Phoenicians, Romans, and Vandals, who named it Anfa.
Portugal destroyed it and had it rebuilt under the name Casa Branca, leaving it only after the earthquake of 1755.
The Sultan of Morocco rebuilt the city under the name Dar al-Baida, and the Spanish merchants gave it the name Casa Blanca, establishing their trading posts here.
Casablanca Port is one of the least preferred places for shopping in Morocco.
Near the old city of Medina, such places can easily be found where traditional Moroccan items such as tagines, clayware, leather goods, hookahs, and various types of toys are sold, but all these items are meant only for tourists.
Goma is a tourist city located in the extreme east of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Rwanda.
In 2002, Goma was destroyed by lava from the Nyiragongo volcano, which engulfed most of the city's roads, particularly those in the city center.
Although Goma is reasonably secure, investigations are necessary to understand the ongoing fighting situation in North Kivu province, even for any movements outside Goma.
This city is also a base for climbing Mount Nyiragongo in the African region, as well as for tracking some fiery mountain gorillas.
You can use a boda boda (motorcycle taxi) to travel around Goma. The normal (local) fare for a short trip is 500 Congolese francs.
