Oiapoque River Bridge is a cable-stayed bridge. It connects the cities of Oiapoque in Brazil and Saint-Georges d'Oiapoque in French Guiana across the Oiapoque River.
The height of these two towers is up to 83 meters, and they are 378 meters long, with both lanes being 3.50 meters wide.
The vertical clearance under the bridge is 15 meters. Its construction was completed in August 2011, and it remained closed to traffic until March 2017.
It has been decided to fully implement it from September 2017, when the Brazilian customs checkpoints are expected to be fully operational.
The Guarani were the most important native group, currently residing as semi-nomadic hunters in eastern Paraguay, practicing agriculture as needed.
The Chaco region was home to other local tribal groups such as the Guaycuru and Payaguá, who lived by hunting, gathering, and fishing.
In the 16th century, Pragoe, which was formerly known as the "Giant Province of the Indies," came into existence as a result of warfare between the local indigenous groups and the Spanish conquerors.
The Spaniards initiated the era of vassalage, which lasted for three centuries.
After the foundation of Asunción in 1537, Paraguay successfully maintained its indigenous character and identity.
Argentina is famous for having some of the best polo teams and players in the world.
